The `present` package: cues and curves for narrated presentations
=================================================================

A presentation is an ordinary Lamlet document, built through `lamlet-doc` with its prose parsed as CommonMark.
Headings are its sections, each paragraph is a sentence to speak, and cues mark where things happen between
the words. This package names those cues and gives the curves that animations follow. The host measures the
speech, and `lamlet present schedule` times every section, sentence, and cue from the measurements.

Nothing here plays, draws, or waits: cues are content, and curves are pure functions of time.

$[Import* std]
$[Import* content]

1. Cues

A cue at a point between words. It happens when the next word is spoken.

$[# {cue name attrs} {anchor name attrs}]

A cue over words: it starts at the first word of `body` and ends after its last. `duringText` takes the words
as a String.

$[# {during name attrs body} {span name attrs body}]
$[# {duringText name attrs s} {span name attrs {text s}}]

An animation over words: like `during`, and the host samples the curve named `curve` at every frame of the cue,
calling it with the seconds since the cue started. The curve is a static top-level binding of type
`Fn([Float], Float)`: a lambda, or a curve computed during the check, `$$[# rise [% {tween 0.0 1.0 2.0 smooth}]]`.
`lamlet present` checks it before any speech is synthesized.

$[# {animate name curve attrs body} {span name {cons {attr "curve" curve} attrs} body}]
$[# {animateText name curve attrs s} {animate name curve attrs {text s}}]

2. Easing, over [0, 1]

$[# {linear t} {clamp t 0.0 1.0}]
$[# {easeIn t} [# x {clamp t 0.0 1.0}] {* x x}]
$[# {easeOut t} [# x {clamp t 0.0 1.0}] {- 1.0 {* {- 1.0 x} {- 1.0 x}}}]

A half cosine, slow at both ends.

$[# {easeInOut t} {- 0.5 {* 0.5 {cos {* pi {clamp t 0.0 1.0}}}}}]

Smootherstep: slow at both ends, with no jerk.

$[# {smooth t} [# x {clamp t 0.0 1.0}] {* x {* x {* x {+ 10.0 {* x {- {* 6.0 x} 15.0}}}}}}]

3. Curves, as functions of seconds

From `a` to `b` over `seconds`, shaped by an easing, and holding `b` after.

$[# {tween a b seconds ease} {^ {t} {lerp a b {ease {/ t seconds}}}}]

A sine wave around `center`.

$[# {wave center amplitude period} {^ {t} {+ center {* amplitude {sin {/ {* 2.0 {* pi t}} period}}}}}]

A constant.

$[# {steady value} {^ {t} value}]
